Description.Recommendations:
An <code>Intent</code> received by a publicly accessible component must be treated as an untrusted data source because it can contain maliciously crafted data. Therefore, do not allow a nested <code>Intent</code> extracted from untrusted <code>Intent</code> to launch a protected Android component.

If possible, change the exposure of the component that extracts and launches the nested <code>Intent</code> from public to private by setting <code>android:exported="false"</code> in <code>AndroidManifest.xml</code>. This ensures that the received <code>Intent</code> originates from your application and can be trusted.

If the component must be publicly accessible, accept a <code>PendingIntent</code> instead of a nested <code>Intent</code>. <code>PendingIntent</code>s are more suitable for inter-application communication because the target component is processed on behalf of the application that created the <code>PendingIntent</code>. Consequently, Android's access control enforcement prevents the processing of a <code>PendingIntent</code> created by an application that does not have access to the target component.

Alternatively, you can validate the nested <code>Intent</code> before redirecting to ensure that it is either sent from a trusted source or targeted for a specific set of allowed components not susceptible to compromise by an untrusted <code>Intent</code>.

<b>Example 2:</b> Verify that the sending activity is from a trusted package.

<pre>
if ("trustedpackage".equals(getCallingActivity().getPackageName())) {
    Intent nextIntent = (Intent) getIntent().getParcelableExtra("next-intent");
    startActivity(nextIntent);
}
</pre>

If you implement validation for the target component, use a destination allow list to verify that the nested <code>Intent</code> can only launch specific components that can safely handle <code>Intent</code>s from untrusted sources. Do not include your application's private components in the allow list to prevent indirect access from malicious applications. If the nested <code>Intent</code> is expected to target an external application, verify that it does not grant URI permissions to protect against privilege escalation.

<b>Example 3:</b> The following code uses an allow list to constrain the <code>Intent</code>'s target to a set of expected components. The example also ensures that URI permissions are not granted in the <code>Intent</code>.

<pre>
Intent nextIntent = (Intent) getIntent().getParcelableExtra("next-intent");
ComponentName componentName = nextIntent.resolveActivity(getPackageManager());
// Validate target component package and class name against allow list
final List&lt;String&gt; allowedComponents = Arrays.asList("trustedcomponent1", "trustedcomponent2");
boolean isAllowed = "trustedpackage".equals(componentName.getPackageName()) &amp;&amp; 
        allowedComponents.contains(componentName.getClassName());
if (!isAllowed) {
    // handle error
} else {
    // Validate that the nested Intent does not grant URI permissions
    int flags = nextIntent.getFlags();
    isAllowed = ((flags &amp; Intent.FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION) == 0) &amp;&amp;
         ((flags &amp; Intent.FLAG_GRANT_WRITE_URI_PERMISSION) == 0);
    if (!isAllowed) {
        // handle error
    } else {
        startActivity(nextIntent);
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
The Android activity allocates a <code>Camera</code> instance that is not released in <code>onPause()</code>, <code>onStop()</code>, or <code>onDestroy()</code> callback. The Android OS invokes these callbacks whenever it needs to send the current activity to the background, or when it needs to temporarily destroy the activity when system resources are low. By failing to release the <code>Camera</code> object properly, the activity prevents other applications (or even future instances of the same application) from accessing the camera. Furthermore, maintaining possession of the <code>Camera</code> instance while the activity is paused can negatively impact the user's experience by unnecessarily draining the battery.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following code describes an Android activity that does not override the base <code>onPause()</code> method, which should be used to release the <code>Camera</code> object, nor does it properly release it during its shutdown sequence.

<pre>
public class UnreleasedCameraActivity extends Activity {
  private Camera cam;

  @Override
  public void onCreate(Bundle state) {
      ...
  }

  @Override
  public void onRestart() {
      ...
  }

  @Override
  public void onStop() {
      cam.stopPreview();
  }
}
</pre>

The Android activity allocates a media object that is not released in <code>onPause()</code>, <code>onStop()</code>, or <code>onDestroy()</code> callback. The Android OS invokes these callbacks whenever it needs to send the current activity to the background, or when it needs to temporarily destroy the activity when system resources are low. By failing to release the media object properly, the activity causes subsequent accesses to Android's media hardware (by other applications or even the same application) to fall back to the software implementations, or even fail altogether. Leaving too many unreleased media instances open can lead Android to throw exceptions, effectively causing a denial of service. Furthermore, maintaining possession of the media instance while the activity is paused can negatively impact the user's experience by unnecessarily draining the battery.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following code describes an Android activity that does not override the base <code>onPause()</code> method, which should be used to release the media object, nor does it properly release it during its shutdown sequence.

<pre>
public class UnreleasedMediaActivity extends Activity {
  private MediaPlayer mp;

  @Override
  public void onCreate(Bundle state) {
      ...
  }

  @Override
  public void onRestart() {
      ...
  }

  @Override
  public void onStop() {
      mp.stop();
  }
}
</pre>

The Android activity maintains an Android SQLite database handler that is not closed in <code>onPause()</code>, <code>onStop()</code>, or <code>onDestroy()</code> callback. The Android OS invokes these callbacks whenever it needs to send the current activity to the background, or when it needs to temporarily destroy the activity when system resources are low. By failing to close the database properly, the activity can potentially exhaust the device of available cursors if the activity is constantly restarted. In addition, depending on the implementation, the Android operating system can also throws <code>DatabaseObjectNotClosedException</code>, which crashes the application if the exception is not caught.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following code describes an Android activity that caches user data and writes the data to disk when the activity is stopped. Note that does not override the base <code>onPause()</code>, which should be used to release the database object, nor does it properly release it during its shutdown sequence.

<pre>
public class MyDBHelper extends SQLiteOpenHelper {
  ...
}

public class UnreleasedDBActivity extends Activity {
  private myDBHelper dbHelper;
  private SQLiteDatabase db;

  @Override
  public void onCreate(Bundle state) {
      ...
      db = dbHelper.getWritableDatabase();
      ...
  }

  @Override
  public void onRestart() {
      ...
  }

  @Override
  public void onStop() {
      db.insert(cached_data);     // flush cached data
  }
}
</pre>

The code attempts to use the <code>Camera</code> object after the it has already been released. Any further references to the <code>Camera</code> object without reacquiring the resource will throw an exception, and can cause the application to crash if the exception is not caught.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following code uses a toggle button to toggle the camera preview on and off. After the user taps the button once, the camera preview stops and the camera resource is released. However, if she taps the button again, <code>startPreview()</code> is called on the previously-released <code>Camera</code> object.

<pre>
public class ReuseCameraActivity extends Activity {
  private Camera cam;

  ...
  private class CameraButtonListener implements OnClickListener {
      public void onClick(View v) {
          if (toggle) {
              cam.stopPreview();
              cam.release();
          }
          else {
              cam.startPreview();
          }
          toggle = !toggle;
      }
  }
  ...
}
</pre>

The code attempts to use the media object after the it has already been released. Any further references to that media object without reacquiring the resource will throw an exception, and can cause the application to crash if the exception is not caught.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following code uses a pause button to toggle the media playback. After the user taps the button once, the current song or video is paused and the camera resource is released. However, if she taps the button again, <code>start()</code> is called on the previously-released media resource.

<pre>
public class ReuseMediaPlayerActivity extends Activity {
  private MediaPlayer mp;

  ...
  private class PauseButtonListener implements OnClickListener {
      public void onClick(View v) {
          if (paused) {
              mp.pause();
              mp.release();
          }
          else {
              mp.start();
          }
          paused = !paused;
      }
  }
  ...
}
</pre>

The code attempts to use the Android SQLite database handler after the it has already been closed. Any further references to the handler without re-establishing the database connection will throw an exception, and can cause the application to crash if the exception is not caught.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following code might be from a program that caches user values temporarily in memory, but can call <code>flushUpdates()</code> to commit the changes to disk. The method properly closes the database handler after writing updates to the database. However, when <code>flushUpdates()</code> is called again, the database object is referenced again before reinitializing it.

<pre>
public class ReuseDBActivity extends Activity {
  private myDBHelper dbHelper;
  private SQLiteDatabase db;

  @Override
  public void onCreate(Bundle state) {
      ...
      db = dbHelper.getWritableDatabase();
      ...
  }
  ...

  private void flushUpdates() {
      db.insert(cached_data);     // flush cached data
      dbHelper.close();
  }
  ...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Access control should be handled in the business and data access layers. Under no circumstances should a user be allowed to retrieve or modify a row in the database without the appropriate permissions. Every query that accesses the database should enforce this policy, which can often be accomplished by simply including the current authenticated username.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following code implements the same functionality as <code>Example 1</code>, but imposes an additional constraint to verify that the invoice belongs to the currently authenticated user.

  <pre>
  ...
  id = this.getIntent().getExtras().getInt("id");
  user = getCurrentAuthenticatedUser();
  cursor = db.query(Uri.parse(invoices), columns, "id = ? AND user = ?", {id, user}, null, null, null);
  ...
  </pre>

The root cause of a query string injection vulnerability is the ability of an attacker to change context in the query, causing a value that the programmer intended to be interpreted as data to be interpreted as a command instead. When a SQLite query is constructed, the programmer knows what should be interpreted as part of the command and what should be interpreted as data. Parameterized queries can enforce this behavior by disallowing data-directed context changes and preventing query string injection attacks. Where user-supplied data needs to be included, include bind parameters, which are placeholders for data that is subsequently inserted. In other words, bind parameters allow the programmer to explicitly specify to the database what should be treated as a command and what should be treated as data. When the program is ready to execute a statement, it specifies to the database the runtime values to use for each of the bind parameters without the risk that the data will be interpreted as a modification to the command.

The <code>SQLiteDatabase</code> class provides the ability to execute parameterized queries using the <code>query()</code> method. The <code>query()</code> method accepts an array of strings, which are bound sequentially to placeholders defined by ?s in the selection filter before the query is executed.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following is the code from <code>Example 1</code> rewritten to use a parameterized query.

  <pre>
  ...
  productCategory = this.getIntent().getExtras().getString("productCategory");
  sortColumn = this.getIntent().getExtras().getString("sortColumn");
  customerID = getAuthenticatedCustomerID(customerName, customerCredentials);
  c = invoicesDB.query(Uri.parse(invoices), columns, "productCategory = ? and customerID = ?", {productCategory, customerID}, null, null, "'" sortColumn + "'asc", null);
  ...
  </pre>

More complicated scenarios, often found in report generation code, require that user input affect the structure of the SQL statement, for instance by adding a dynamic constraint in the <code>WHERE</code> clause. Do not use this requirement to justify concatenating user input to create a query string. Prevent SQL injection attacks where user input must affect command structure with a level of indirection: create a set of legitimate strings that correspond to different elements you might include in a SQL statement. When constructing a statement, use input from the user to select from this set of application-controlled values.

The root cause of a query string injection vulnerability is the ability of an attacker to change context in the query, causing a value that the programmer intended to be interpreted as data to be interpreted as a command instead. When a SQLite query is constructed, the programmer knows what should be interpreted as part of the command and what should be interpreted as data. Parameterized queries can enforce this behavior by disallowing data-directed context changes and preventing query string injection attacks. Where user-supplied data needs to be included, include bind parameters, which are placeholders for data that is subsequently inserted. In other words, bind parameters allow the programmer to explicitly specify to the database what should be treated as a command and what should be treated as data. When the program is ready to execute a statement, it specifies to the database the runtime values to use for each of the bind parameters without the risk that the data will be interpreted as a modification to the command.

The <code>SQLiteDatabase</code> class provides the ability to execute parameterized queries using the <code>query()</code> method. The <code>query()</code> method accepts an array of strings, which are bound sequentially to placeholders defined by ?s in the selection filter before the query is executed.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following is the code from <code>Example 1</code> rewritten to use a parameterized query.

  <pre>
  ...
